===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|armsful|armsfuls}}

# The [[amount#Noun|amount]] an [[arm#Noun|arm]] or arms can [[hold#Verb|hold]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Joseph Blagrave|[Joseph Blagrave]]]|chapter=How to Order an Orchard that It shall Never Misse Bearing|title=New Additions to the Art of Husbandry. [...]|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for}} Benjamin Billingsley,{{nb...|and are to be sold at the sign of the Printing-Presse in Broad-street.}}|year=1670|page=45|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=wxR2Y_fgq-UC&pg=PA45|oclc=35245904|passage=[T]ake three or four good arms full of muckle Straw, Hay, or Fern, not too wet, not to dry, and obſerving which ſide of the Orchard the Wind blows on, then laying a good '''armful''' of muckle in three or four places according to the bigneſs of your Orchard, then get ſome dry ſticks, and having kindled them put an '''armful''' of muckle upon the Fire, and it will ſmoak and [[smother|ſmoother]], and the wind will drive the ſmoak through the whole Orchard, continue it till the wind turn out of the Eaſterly quarter, and it will preſerve the Trees and Fruit from [[blights|blites]] and all manner of flys and caterpillers, which thoſe ſorts of bliting winds uſually bring; [...]}}
#* {{RQ:L'Estrange Fables of Aesop|fablenumber=CCLXXXI|fable=A Sick Hermit|page=245|passage=Upon this, by Conſent, the Doctors put a Good '''Armful''' of Warm Womans Fleſh into the Bed to him, [...]}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Richard Bellings|R[ichard] B[ellings]]]|chapter=A Sixth Book to [[w:The Countess of Pembroke's Arcadia|The Countess of Pembroke’s Arcadia]]|mainauthor=w:Philip Sidney|title=The Works of the Honourable Sir Philip Sidney, Kt.|location=London|publisher=[s.n.]|year=1724|volume=III|page=53|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=KsUIAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA53|oclc=663665444|passage=[T]hough ''Pyrocles'' and ''Muſidorus'' at other times would diſpence with the length of the ſports, yet now, in reſpect of the '''armfuls''' of joys they were to expect in bed, they thought them tedious; [...]}}
#* {{RQ:Dictionaire Oeconomique|entry=PLOVER|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=k9A_AQAAMAAJ&pg=PP252|column=2|passage=The Lodge is to be made after this Manner; [...] You muſt lay a good Handful of Straw under the Cord upon the Turf, to prevent dirting the Cord, as well as ſpoiling the Turf, and you had need to have a good '''Armful''' in your Lodge, to keep you warm and dry, as Occaſion requires; [...]}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Kate Mont Albion [pseudonym; Catharine Bayley]|title=A Set-down at Court; {{...|Including a Series of Anecdotes in High Life, and the History of Monthemar. A Novel, Founded on Fact.}} In Four Volumes|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed and published by}} Allen & Co.,{{nb...|No. 15, Paternoster-Row.}}|year=1812|volume=I|pages=30–31|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=PfvuKibSn58C&pg=PA31|oclc=778245242|passage=[I]t was thus the passion of Mr. Panton allayed its overflowings: for very shortly after his marriage, ''he'' again ''fell passionately in love'' with another lady; a ''bona roba Queen'', the full head taller than himself, and more than an '''armfull'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Our Library Table|magazine=[[w:The Athenaeum (British magazine)|The Athenæum: Journal of Literature, Science, and the Fine Arts]]|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed by James Holmes, Took’s Court, Chancery Lane. Published at the office, 14, Wellington Street, Strand, by}} J[ohn] Francis. [...]|date=1 July 1837|issue=505|page=481|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=UI5UAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA481|column=3|oclc=956082422|passage=We have here a handful—an '''armful''' would be nearer the truth—on poems on scriptural and sacred subjects.}}
#* {{RQ:Dana Two Years|chapter=XXVI|page=286|passage=In this state of the weather, and before sunrise, in the grey of the morning, we had to wade off, nearly up to our hips in water, to load the skiff with the wood by '''armsfull'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Julian Silveyra|title=Appendix No. 9. Method of Cultivation and Cure of Cuba Tobacco, as Practised in Cuba and the Other West India Islands.|translator=James S. Peacocke|journal=Annual Report of the Commissioner of Patents for the Year 1847|series=30th [[w:United States Congress|Congress]], 1st Session, [[w:United States House of Representatives|House of Representatives]]|seriesvolume=ex. doc. no. 54|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher={{...|Printed by}} Wendell and Van Benthuysen|date=25 August 1846|year_published=3 March 1848|page=436|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=5PwWAQAAIAAJ&pg=PA436|issn=0083-3002|oclc=144619337|passage=In the morning they [sticks of tobacco] should be gathered up and placed in '''armfuls''' in a box made of twigs or slats from whence they should be taken for assorting, which should be in done a sheltered place.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Aunt Becky’s Load of Wood|magazine=[[w:The United States Magazine and Democratic Review|The United States Magazine]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=J. M. Emerson & Co.|month=August|year=1856|volume=III|issue=2|pages=153–154|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=kGwjPk5aoZsC&pg=PA153|oclc=977506194|passage="Oh, yes, yes, Mary; give Aunt Becky a few sticks of wood, so she can get through with her washing." / "Well, how many sticks, father?" / "Oh, give her an '''armful'''; give her an '''armful''', my child." / "An '''armful''', father? what, my '''armful''' or her '''armful'''?" / "Why her '''armful''', of course, child." / "Well, how big an '''armful'''? or how many sticks, father?" / "Why, my child, give her as much as she can carry in her arms."}}
#* {{RQ:Stowe Poganuc People|chapter=Dissolving Views|pages=8–9|pageref=8|passage=Oh, Nabby, Nabby! do tell me what they are doing up at your church. I’ve seen ’em all day carrying '''armfulls''' and '''armfulls'''—ever so much—spruce and pine up that way, and Jim Brace and Tom Peters told me they were going to have a ’lumination there, and when I asked what a ’lumination was they only laughed at me and called me a Presbyterian.}}
#* {{RQ:Milne Not That It Matters|chapter=My Library|page=12|passage=Together we hurried the books into the new white shelves which awaited them, the order in which they stood being of no matter so long as they were off the floor. '''Armful''' after '''armful''' was hastily stacked, the only pause being when (in the curious way in which these things happen) my own name suddenly caught the eye of the foreman. "Did you write this one, sir?" he asked. I admitted it.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Tokyo Bomber Hero is Italian Prisoner|editor=George Starr Lasher|magazine=The Rattle of Theta Chi|location=Athens, Oh.|publisher=[[w:Theta Chi|Theta Chi Fraternity]]|month=September|year=1943|volume=XXXII|issue=1|page=30|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=ENLnAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA30|column=3|oclc=16619918|passage=I left the plane with two '''armfuls''' of groceries, a gun, searchlight, and other things. It wasn't until I was 500 feet down that I realized the problem of pulling the ripcord with my arms full.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|editor=Adele Sarkissian|title=Something About the Author|series=Studies in Austrian Literature, Culture, and Thought; Autobiography Series|seriesvolume=1|location=Detroit, Mich.|publisher=[[w:Gale (publisher)|Gale Research Co.]]|month=December|year=1986|page=66|isbn=978-0-8103-4452-5|passage=When Vernon came in from work, he always brought me wildflowers he had found, an '''armsful''' of tiger lilies, bluebells, and white daisies.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=w:Film Culture|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Film Culture|year=1992|page=23|issn=0015-1211|oclc=819011324|passage={{w|Naomi Levine}} made a movie wherein little kids swatted each other with '''armsfuls''' of huge white flowers.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Aleks Sierz|chapter=|title=Rewriting the Nation: British Theatre Today|location=London|publisher=Methuen Drama, [[w:A & C Black|A & C Black Publishers]]|year=2011|page=212|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=67QDyz4jDkoC&pg=PA212|isbn=978-1-408-11238-0|passage=At one point, a man who looks after lost property for London Transport asks himself what the ‘'''armfuls''' and '''armfuls'''’ of mislaid umbrellas say about Britain: ‘It says caution, it says preparation, it says pragmatism. Quiet, ordered lives in which nothing could be more distressing than to get rained on. The umbrella, the safety net of the nation’ [...].}}

